lighten mine eyes—dim with weakness,
denoting approaching death (compare 1Sa 14:27-29; Ps 6:7;
38:10).

my soul—or self (compare Ps 3:2;
16:10).

my darling—literally, "my only one,"
or, "solitary one," as desolate and afflicted (Ps 25:16;
35:17).

the secret of thy presence—or,
covering of Thy countenance; the protection He thus affords;
compare Ps
27:5 for a similar figure;
"dwelling" used there for "presence" here. The idea of security further
presented by the figure of a tent and a fortified city [Ps 31:21].

False witnesses—literally,
"Witnesses of injustice and cruelty" (compare Ps 11:5;
25:19).

13, 14. He patiently submits, uttering no
reproaches or replies (Joh 19:9) to
their insulting speeches;

under us—that is, His saints;
Israel's temporal victories were types of the spiritual conquests of
the true Church.

God reconciled, material sacrifices
will be acceptable (Ps 4:5;
compare Isa 1:11-17).

all-devouring—literally,
"swallowing," which utterly destroy (compare Ps 21:9;
35:25).

shall … fear—regard with
religious awe.

laugh at him—for his folly;

God is for me—or, "on my side" (Ps 118:6;
124:1, 2); hence he is sure
of the repulse of his foes.

6, 7. the king—himself and his royal
line ending in Christ. Mercy and truth personified, as in Ps 40:11;
57:3.

holdeth … in life—literally,
"putteth our soul in life"; that is, out of danger (Ps 30:3;
49:15).

to be moved—(Compare Ps 10:6;
55:22).

3-5. Thanks will be rendered for the
blessings of His wise and holy government (compare Isa 2:3, 4;
11:4).

16-18. These earnest terms are often used, and
the address to God, as indifferent or averse, is found in Ps 3:7; 22:24;
27:9, &c.

gave—or, "shut up."

his people—(Ps 78:48;
1Sa 4:10-17).

We need quickening grace (Ps 71:20;
119:25) to persevere in Thy
right worship (Ge 4:26; Ro 10:11).

By the wilful ignorance and negligence of
judges, anarchy ensues (Ps 11:3; 75:3).

out of course—(Compare Margin;
Ps 9:6;
62:2).

12-14. The vigorous growth, longevity,
utility, fragrance, and beauty of these noble trees, set forth the
life, character, and destiny of the pious;

Lift up thyself—or, "Arise," both
figures representing God as heretofore indifferent (compare Ps 3:7;
22:16, 20).

7, 8. which condescension is illustrated as
often in raising the worthy poor and needy to honor (compare 1Sa 2:8;
Ps 44:25).

BETH. (Ps 119:9-16).

9. The whole verse may be read as a question;
for,

by taking heed—is better, "for" taking
heed, that is, so as to do it. The answer is implied, and inferable
from Ps 119:5, 10, 18, &c., that is, by God's grace.

The communion of the saints. Delight in
their company is an evidence of belonging to them (Ps 16:3;
Am 3:3; Mal 3:16).

3, 4. His sleepless vigilance is added.

to be moved—(Compare Ps 38:16;
66:9).

the cords—that is, which fasten the
plough to the ox; and cutting denotes God's arresting the
persecution;

out of Zion—(Compare Ps 110:2;
134:3). From the Church, as a
center, His praise is diffused throughout the earth.

2-5. This character of the wicked, and the
devices planned against the pious, correspond to Ps 10:7;
31:13; 58:4, &c.
